#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134834} :activity/id #uuid "6fb1a6a6-6d39-5ea2-af30-b6e13fa3c1ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135110 :tutorial/content "When considering an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ as a linear transformation\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^n \\xrightarrow{\\quad A \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^m,\n$$\n\nthere are **four fundamental subspaces** that naturally occur. The first two are as follows:\n\n- The **column space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ This is the set of all linear combinations of the columns of $A.$\n\n- The **null space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is the set of all vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that, when pre-multiplied by $A,$ result in the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nNow, notice that the transpose $A^T$ is an $n \\times m$ matrix and can be considered as the transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbb{R}^m \\xrightarrow{\\quad A^T \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nSo, we have similar subspaces for the transpose $A^T{:}$\n\n- The column space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ We call this the **row space** of $A$ since the span of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to the span of the rows of $A.$\n\n- The null space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ We call this the **left null space** of $A$ since it is equivalent to the set of all vectors $\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^m$ such that $\\mathbf y^T A = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's discuss how these spaces relate to each other:\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that are orthogonal complements of each other:\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A^T) = \\text{Null}(A)^\\perp\n$$\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ that are orthogonal complements of each other:\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A) = \\text{Null}(A^T)^\\perp\n$$\n\nThe four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ced4191955b6886491bf612c62344d2faf6e7fe8d49884446de0eb86851d22e8.png)\n\nTo see why $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ note the following:\n\n- When calculating the product $A^T \\mathbf x,$ we multiply each row of $A^T$ by $\\mathbf x.$\n\n- Suppose that $\\mathbf x\\in \\text{Null}(A^T).$ Then, by definition, $A^T \\mathbf x = \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Thus, $\\mathbf x$ must be orthogonal to each row of $A^T.$ Equivalently, this means that $\\mathbf x$ is orthogonal to each *column* of $A.$\n\n- Hence, $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nWe can deduce that $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ by similar reasoning." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b6f45b1-7cfa-5c62-9928-caeff36d1cc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f59f0795-5e5a-517e-8142-7251b3810d58" :step/math-academy-id 33903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133701 :question/id #uuid "47945f1d-ab72-5fe8-bc2c-43a69480b323" :question/math-academy-id "e-9090"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476895}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a063cde8-8349-5bde-8239-ea3b6c3146eb" :step/math-academy-id 33904 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133703 :question/id #uuid "bb597435-f43e-54de-a219-1af646efcb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-9099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a36c7f7-4287-562b-94ea-047b38ede70f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476871}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f05c128d-c2b3-55a6-b5af-aa01107d4052" :step/math-academy-id 33918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133704 :question/id #uuid "bd1f48b4-8bfc-536d-8145-0effcf44a788" :question/math-academy-id "e-9104"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495139}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"} :step/id #uuid "f70134b1-0b93-5597-a6b6-16fdc7fb1bad" :step/math-academy-id 33937 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135111 :tutorial/content "Given an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ what is the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under $A?$\n\nNote that pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$\n![](images/a3/a3cf2fb6734efe387ad41c59743385154d7a172d2cc10ee0fe69ac6a7f2b342a.svg)\n\nTherefore, by definition, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$\n\n![](images/db/dbefb436195af5ffa8841f4119054426db50ac2987138a5912991cf42321e0dc.png)\n\nSimilarly, pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$ by $A^T$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A^T{:}$\n![](images/60/60574ae7f9fc09522e9d725c787dce63228c67e19cbef002b14e29c1f27e4b20.svg)\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ under the action of $A^T$ must be $\\text{Col}(A^T).$ This is also the row space of $A,$ since a linear combination of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to a linear combination of the rows of $A.$\n\n![](images/c8/c82ab88289fc2d35ce354933937d908c54a9839bfa0fd126187a9723c58eeb27.png)\n\nWe can use similar reasoning to find the image of the four fundamental subspaces under the appropriate matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "915420e9-f241-5e99-8274-32ffdfb79b9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5454 :tutorial/title "Images Under the Action of a Matrix and Its Transpose"} :step/id #uuid "e8b74e81-7593-5f0a-9136-22383330a688" :step/math-academy-id 33950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135112 :tutorial/content "Consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with rank $r.$ The dimensions of the four fundamental subspaces of $A$ are as follows:\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) = r.$ Since the column space is the span of the columns of $A,$ its dimension is the number of linearly independent columns which, by Gaussian elimination, is $\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)) = r.$ It can be shown that the dimension of the row space of $A$ also equals $r.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(A^T)=\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = n-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)}_{n} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))}_{r} = n-r.\n$$\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = m-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^m)}_{m} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))}_{r} = m-r.\n$$\n\nLet's add these dimensions to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c550b87048bba6a7c362f00f09faca74ca9b056a93698436d0994c120971356.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "40af35b2-abb6-59b8-999a-9976edbb9ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5455 :tutorial/title "Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "957cde37-32d4-515e-ad96-75c96f7bfa57" :step/math-academy-id 33951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135113 :tutorial/content "Let's review what we have learned in this lesson.\n\nIf we consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ of rank $r$ as a transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ then there are four fundamental subspaces of $A{:}$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ with dimensions $r$ and $m-r,$ respectively.\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ and $\\text{Null}(A)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with dimensions $r$ and $n-r,$ respectively.\n\nFurthermore,\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ and\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$ and $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A)$ under the action of $A^T$ is $\\text{Col}(A^T).$\n\nThese facts about the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/37/37f633d0bca1a599f7d1658c051f802da5d607d420beeb4bddbef5cd98c366ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "190e1777-4f27-5c94-88bd-21c9992eb024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5456 :tutorial/title "The Big Picture of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "8eef352e-273a-5614-b97d-8c357807e9a0" :step/math-academy-id 33952}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134041}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248} :activity/id #uuid "d12b0c27-35c4-517e-8eb3-41dab352b8fa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e37cdb6-c2f8-5f2d-b68e-1ab7dae2c068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472502} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ee36b-6930-5033-bbff-c6e5831b6de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472503}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472502} :answer-field/id #uuid "48015e6d-6aef-53c6-aa13-f63167aef2d9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c132e320-a78b-5003-a32b-1274e70dff6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472504} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9107f0-649f-5e5e-8c94-d1089de849cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472505}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472505} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff283957-040e-592b-8540-faef6177d2d1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49c7e552-c7ec-5bd5-9721-98ed03dbe1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472506} {:answer/id #uuid "bce36cf7-e320-5e6d-97a5-94061b6e55fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472506} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2b47b3-71ab-5d28-ac5e-96cdddfab7f6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498" :question/math-academy-id "q-109777" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A)$ for every matrix $A?$\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n- **II.** $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $A$, that is, the rows of ${A}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${A}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${A}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "453b68d4-1d9d-50c9-a90c-51c8b99946a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad86372-161b-56b5-b9b3-67cb9855544f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407216} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4b4700-8836-57b3-a254-6685beac57ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407217} {:answer/id #uuid "65001b28-0435-5b9d-91ec-614c84c822e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407221} {:answer/id #uuid "bf467989-30ff-5c80-94cc-972d1936e437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407228} {:answer/id #uuid "d63461e1-95f1-5efd-aec4-a9f50c64386c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407228} :answer-field/id #uuid "32cd5975-d7c1-5dd8-91de-4834a553c50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9c49e80-5070-56aa-b070-99b1fa2fdff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109848" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $m\\times n$ matrix. What is the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nPre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{m}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the row space of $A.$"} :step/id #uuid "6ef8f97d-5b2a-5ab0-998f-92729ef9dc31" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166f24b3-2b33-5589-9ba5-efd73efdf696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407215} {:answer/id #uuid "794d7c6a-4727-5c98-8506-efa641e24334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407222} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f31e2f-2172-581c-b46f-338027d98c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407229} {:answer/id #uuid "de4a68e5-c938-5191-8ef4-522e158bedb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407233} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d32fbe-6d2e-5aca-9070-ed1ab0efc4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407229} :answer-field/id #uuid "077cd26a-5956-5d07-8539-e101ab53d7f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d75070f-88dd-5b20-8fec-c1f3c6de013c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109846" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 4$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=3,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=5,$ $n=4,$ and $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) & =n-r=4-3=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f121dc0-6e9f-506e-8370-6a73e7b21516" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0fd37b-833c-5581-bf94-abc93beffe65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407214} {:answer/id #uuid "6203c04e-a6ad-568e-b086-f63c9a0600d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407220} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba999d-5dc0-5e42-af53-78dc73ee4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407224} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9ba337-a858-55b1-bf28-9f915ba4dcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407226} {:answer/id #uuid "e18de25a-171d-5acb-88a3-6812297610fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407226} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82e089a-caba-5439-8d15-a646e7c8e9fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "66ca173a-d952-58d1-b484-502590c17693" :question/math-academy-id "q-109873" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=3,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T}))=$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=5,$ $n=6,$ and $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T})) & =m-r=5-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9114f2aa-68cd-5878-a5bf-ad32cd59fd29"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3a4ec2-bd1c-569f-bc25-a735fbf6e8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407213} {:answer/id #uuid "4624e774-8f8e-50d7-922a-9f2511cb84dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Null}(AB)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407218} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8461c5-2db5-555b-98b8-ef3d5aadb4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({B}^{T}{A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407225} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1a752d-a1b9-5c75-a6eb-005f23fda936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({B}^{T}{A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407230} {:answer/id #uuid "cafe991c-8437-5728-ad05-fe57c0cef2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(AB)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407231} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14d9fc2-b7d8-5af8-b07b-319f63dc6ba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "de769de3-3ab7-5536-ae28-2dcbd744b48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109861" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be $m\\times p$ and $p\\times n$ matrices, respectively. What is the image of $\\text{Col}({B}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})$ under the action of $AB?$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $AB$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $AB:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ (ab)_{1} & (ab)_{2} & ⋯ & (ab)_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{AB}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (ab)_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (ab)_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (ab)_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $AB$ must be $\\text{Col}(AB),$ the column space of $AB.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(AB)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}((AB)^{T})=\\text{Col}({B}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(AB).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/885bf8a3061f93ad400897a7273ffe4157d22dee20b8e4ee7ebb982b1c4319bb.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d8a9f51b-41b7-5b08-9832-d19d696012d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407251}}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407212}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495175} :activity/id #uuid "9ad0665c-4138-59ae-93cc-e6c2dc9739c7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495175 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8967c8-7a09-5aca-be92-070777e639f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362638} {:answer/id #uuid "ae42776d-9890-5f6d-ba04-a42b0f4d6b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362639} {:answer/id #uuid "14529e7e-b092-5d31-b4ed-2ba06143f782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921